WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.636
-You know my first guest from
the series "The Good Place"

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:05.572
and as Princess Anna
from "Frozen."

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:07.573
She is the best.
She has a new children's book

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:10.409
called "The World
Needs More Purple People,"

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:12.011
which is out now.

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:13.746
Here is Kristen Bell!

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:15.348
Yeah! Aww. Look at you.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.083
Oh, my God.
You are the greatest. Come on.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.817
Thank you so --
-We'll be the audience. Hi!

00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:20.319
-Yeah. Thank you
for being the au--

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:22.655
Hi. Thank you so much
for coming on the show.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:24.657
Congrats on the book,
by the way.

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:27.160
Number one. Not too shabby.
-Thank you.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.429
-Quickly,
before we get into this,

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:30.930
thank you so much.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.532
You sent the nicest
video message

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:35.735
to my 5-year-old turning 6
on her birthday,

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:37.904
and it changed her life.

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:41.707
She was -- You were just --
Not only did you say hi,

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:44.711
but you really put time into it
and, like, spent a lot of time

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:46.545
with my daughter,
and she watched it forever,

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:48.014
and all she did
was talk about it,

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.249
and she said it was
the best birthday of her life.

00:00:50.249 --> 00:00:53.719
She was like,
"Princess Anna and I --

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:55.755
The only thing --
We have a lot in common,

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.024
but the one thing
we don't have in common,

00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:00.093
we don't --
she doesn't like to swim."

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:02.428
All she could do was talk
about you, talk about you.

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:05.864
And, anyways, me and my wife
just -- We're obsessed with you.

00:01:05.864 --> 00:01:08.067
You're a good egg, as they say.
-That makes me so happy.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.702
That makes me so, so happy.

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.839
-That was so cool of you to do.
I know how busy you are.

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:15.141
You probably get
a thousand requests a day,

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.545
but that was just --
Honestly, we need --

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:21.113
The world needs
more Kristen Bells.

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:23.383
Let's just say that.

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.718
That's the sequel.
That's the sequel book.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:28.120
We both have daughters
around the same age.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:32.157
What kind of things
are your girls into right now?

00:01:32.157 --> 00:01:33.526
-Right now, well, with --

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:35.495
I mean, you know,
with quarantine,

00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:36.929
what I've actually been doing --

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:39.298
They've been doing nothing
but roller skating.

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:41.768
I'm apparently raising
roller-skating champions.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.135
-Wow.
-They love it.

00:01:43.135 --> 00:01:45.438
They are on them
all day every day,

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:48.107
and they can do the, like,
around-the-world

00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:49.709
where they, like,
hold their foot out.

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:52.345
-No! That thing. Yeah. I know.
The leg kick out and then --

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.947
Like that?
-It's sick! It's sick.

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.949
Their balance is sick.
I don't know where they get it.

00:01:56.949 --> 00:01:59.151
And they know they're not
supposed to do it in the house,

00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:01.421
but they do,
and our wood floors are wrecked.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:04.723
But show me someone in 2020

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:06.625
whose wood floors
aren't wrecked.

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:08.294
-Thank you.
-You know what I mean?

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:11.797
-I have to imagine that you were
a pretty good kid growing up.

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:14.933
Am I correct or no?
-I was. Oh, yeah.

00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:17.770
I have a spotless record.
-Right?

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.707
-Well, except --
Okay. I was a very good kid.

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:25.811
I was, like, a goodie-goodie
except, when I got to college --

00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:28.982
I've always been
a bit of an entrepreneur,

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:33.786
and I noticed that, in 1998,
the New Jersey --

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:36.755
You remember this, by the way,
'cause we were both in New York.

00:02:36.755 --> 00:02:39.558
The New Jersey licenses
were literally a piece of paper

00:02:39.558 --> 00:02:41.194
with lamination over it.

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:44.630
So in college,
I made and sold fake IDs.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.232
-Fake IDs. Really?

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:49.669
-I did.
I sold them for $20 a pop.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.405
-That's not bad.
That's the going rate probably.

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.907
Wait. You're younger than me.

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:56.542
But did you
have to print it out?

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:58.778
Did you have a printer?
Where would you do this?

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:00.012
-Yes.
-Used the school?

00:03:00.012 --> 00:03:02.515
-I printed it out on --
Not the school.

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:04.016
'Cause I had a desktop.

00:03:04.016 --> 00:03:05.885
My roommate and I shared
a desktop. We had a desktop.

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.353
And so I'd print it out.

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:09.555
Then I'd just get
lamination paper and take --

00:03:09.555 --> 00:03:13.325
And there was sort of this
weird wink-wink happening

00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:15.028
around the campus of NYU.

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:17.163
I would sell them to my friends,

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:19.698
and all the bars around NYU,
when you were 19,

00:03:19.698 --> 00:03:23.302
they would see it, and I would
have to imagine they knew

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:27.239
because it was, like, you know,
a terrible fake ID replica.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:31.110
But they kind of were like,
"I'll give you a beer with this,

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.645
and you better be able
to walk out of here,

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:34.480
but, like, we're not
getting drunk on it.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.416
This is going to be
a one-time pass."

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:41.821
You do silly things
when you're a kid.

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:45.057
And wait.
What year did you start "SNL"?

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:48.194
-'98.
-'98.

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:53.366
Do you ever remember
meeting me there?

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:56.502
I knew you didn't. That's okay.
So, listen.

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:59.205
So, when I was in college --

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.173
This is a major
blast from the past.

00:04:01.173 --> 00:04:04.343
When I was in college,
Ariel Ashe,

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:07.012
who was one of my best friends
in college, who was the --

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:09.215
I know. Hello. I know.

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:11.417
-I love Ariel Ashe.

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:15.088
-The assistant -- the intern
in the set department...

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:16.222
-Yes.

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:17.690
-...to Landon,
I think his name was.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.658
And so obviously
I went there every weekend

00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:21.493
and would, like,
walk around the halls.

00:04:21.493 --> 00:04:25.864
But mind you,
I looked maybe 13, okay?

00:04:25.864 --> 00:04:27.533
I was 18,
but I was definitely like --

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:29.969
-This is a photo of you
from that time.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.271
-That's a photo of me.
Right. Exactly.

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:34.006
That was my ID at the time.

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:36.609
I blew it up and put it
on the back of the book.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.311
-Ariel Ashe is the best.

00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:41.113
-But that's where I first
became friends with Mike Schur.

00:04:41.113 --> 00:04:44.116
Because, I mean, look. You were
on the show. You were very cool.

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:48.487
But, like, Mike, who was also
a little baby-boy writer there,

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:51.390
that's how I first
became friends with him.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.926
-And you ended up doing
"The Good Place."

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.862
Wow. That is --
-Cut to "The Good Place."

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:57.996
Isn't that nuts?

00:04:57.996 --> 00:05:00.733
-And Ariel's sister
is married to Seth Meyers.

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:04.103
-Seth. Exactly. Alexi. I know.
We have so many "connec-shees."

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:06.438
-That's wild.
Oh, I love that story.

00:05:06.438 --> 00:05:08.574
-I know.
-Thanks for telling me.

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:11.410
I want to talk to you
about your new children's book,

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.147
"The World Needs
More Purple People."

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.048
I love the idea about this book.

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:18.751
It's the number-one
"New York Times" best seller.

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:21.821
So congrats on that.
-Thank you very much.

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:23.722
-How'd the idea come about?

00:05:23.722 --> 00:05:26.258
And I think you're
a great person to do this,

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:27.793
because I like

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:29.495
"Are you kind? Are you curious?

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.763
Can you be really, really
ridiculously silly?"

00:05:31.763 --> 00:05:34.833
I kind of think that you do --
-Yes, yes, yes.

00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:37.002
-I think you do
all these things. Yeah.

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:38.571
That's very on-brand.
-I try.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.808
You know, purple is obviously
a little bit of a metaphor.

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:44.344
It's not, you know,
super under-the-radar.

00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:46.679
But we --
One of my other best friends,

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:49.315
Ben Hart and I, we were having
a family dinner two years ago.

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:51.050
You just wrote
a children's book,

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.551
so you know
it takes a long time,

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:54.120
even though
they're only 20 pages.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.888
-Two years. Yeah.
-It takes like two years.

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:57.189
-Yeah. I agree.
-Right.

00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:59.959
So we were sitting around
the table, and we were just

00:05:59.959 --> 00:06:03.196
sort of noticing this very
polarizing political culture

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:05.598
seep into our kid's daily lives,

00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:07.599
and we were like,
"This feels wrong.

00:06:07.599 --> 00:06:10.369
It feels like we're seeing
enemies everywhere."

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:13.906
And so we wanted to do something
helpful with the language

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:16.142
that they were even
subliminally digesting

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:17.710
when they saw the television

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.545
and they saw red versus blue,
blue versus red.

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:22.214
And I was like,
"It's not 'versus' anything."

00:06:22.214 --> 00:06:25.183
And so I wanted to sort of
create a social identity

00:06:25.183 --> 00:06:27.987
that positions our kids
towards other human beings,

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:31.891
to work out any disagreements,
any different opinions.

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:34.761
So, like, we came up
with five great pillars

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:36.528
that we thought
no one could argue with.

00:06:36.528 --> 00:06:38.130
Like, what would
everyone agree on?

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.499
So we were like,
"Ask great questions,

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:43.502
laugh a lot, use your voice,
work hard, and also just be

00:06:43.502 --> 00:06:47.673
the unique you and celebrate
all the differences."

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:50.309
Because we were like -- I don't
think I could find a person

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:51.777
on the planet that was like,

00:06:51.777 --> 00:06:54.747
"It's really bad to laugh a lot.
You shouldn't do it."

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:56.115
-Yeah. Exactly.
-You know?

00:06:56.115 --> 00:06:59.384
-Look at the drawings.
The art is fantastic.

00:06:59.384 --> 00:07:01.187
-You know what's so funny is --

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.322
When I was reading this
to my children --

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:05.457
We talk a lot about
being a purple person

00:07:05.457 --> 00:07:08.928
'cause, to us, it just is this
kind, curious human being

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:10.729
that you can be in the world.

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:12.198
And I read them the book,

00:07:12.198 --> 00:07:14.499
and they were just, like,
not interested.

00:07:14.499 --> 00:07:17.336
And I was like, "Uh-oh.
I've done something wrong."

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:19.338
And I was like,
"No. Wait a minute.

00:07:19.338 --> 00:07:23.208
They're just not interested in
this Google Doc on my computer.

00:07:23.208 --> 00:07:24.710
They need art."

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.846
So when Daniel Wiseman
came on board,

00:07:26.846 --> 00:07:28.581
who drew those amazing pictures,

00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:30.582
then it changed,
and then they really liked it.

00:07:30.582 --> 00:07:33.386
But we're very, very proud
of sort of what the book means

00:07:33.386 --> 00:07:36.455
and how we can celebrate
being unique

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:39.124
but all live
in the same place together.

00:07:39.124 --> 00:07:41.093
-You've got a winner here, pal.
Congrats on this.

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:42.628
When we come back...
-Thank you.

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:45.097
-...I heard that you might have
a Christmas song prepared.

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.132
Is that...
-I do, Jimmy.

00:07:47.132 --> 00:07:49.568
I have something
very, very special for you.

00:07:49.568 --> 00:07:51.770
And it's the first time
I'm sharing it,

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:54.506
and I'm excited
to bring it to your ears.

00:07:54.506 --> 00:07:56.742
-I cannot wait. More with
Kristen Bell after the break.

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:58.744
Stick around, everybody!

